From 4614515804c057034763ed7f1b5c2a79c671f7a1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 05:21:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] ExactMeshBoolean: the classification's exact sign stages
 on stack limbs (ExactLimbSigns)

The plane side A X + B Y + C Z - D W, the dot of two normals and the 4 x 4 orientation of four
homogeneous points were BigInteger expressions: every product and sum a heap object. On the
slow tail (open, heavily intersecting inputs, whose pieces are classified one by one) they are a
large part of the operation and most of its allocation.

ExactLimbSigns evaluates the same expressions with FixedArith on stack limbs: the operands are
copied from their BigInteger storage per call, products and sums live in a stack arena, only
the sign leaves. The same integers, so the same signs and the same results; no allocation.
Operands wider than 40 limbs and netstandard2.0 keep the BigInteger expression.

Call sites: interior_side, carried_crossings (two normal dots), local_side, side,
front_crossings (two), orient3d. The filters before the stages and the control flow are
unchanged.

Tests: the kernels against BigInteger over all widths, the scratch space at the limit, no
allocation; the operation with the filters off gives the filtered mesh and allocates far less
(fails on the base). Mutation: the kernels join the shard mesh-fixed; the offsets of the
mesh-boolean shards are moved back to their methods.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
